WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.733
Common telemetry sources include operating systems, endpoints, applications,

00:00:03.733 --> 00:00:07.820
identity providers, network devices, cloud services, databases, infrastructure platforms,

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.182
and security controls.

00:00:09.182 --> 00:00:15.540
Each source offers a different view of activity and has its own strengths, gaps,

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.356
formats, and retention characteristics.

00:00:17.356 --> 00:00:22.351
Identity systems can show sign-ins, authentication failures, privilege changes, token activity,

00:00:22.351 --> 00:00:23.714
and account administration.

00:00:23.714 --> 00:00:27.346
Applications may record requests, errors, transactions, administrative actions,

00:00:27.346 --> 00:00:29.617
and access to sensitive functions.

00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:33.704
Network devices can describe communications between systems and locations.

00:00:33.704 --> 00:00:39.153
Security tools such as endpoint detection and response platforms, firewalls, email gateways,

00:00:39.153 --> 00:00:44.602
and cloud security services often enrich raw events with classifications or alerts.

00:00:44.602 --> 00:00:48.235
A SIEM can centralize and correlate these sources,

00:00:48.235 --> 00:00:54.139
but it can only analyze the data that is actually collected and understood.

00:00:54.139 --> 00:00:58.226
Collection planning begins with security questions and important assets.

00:00:58.226 --> 00:01:02.767
Teams identify which activity must be observable, select authoritative sources,

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:04.583
document ownership and dependencies,

00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:10.941
and confirm that expected events arrive with enough context to support detection and investigation.

00:01:10.941 --> 00:01:14.573
Maintaining a telemetry catalog makes this work repeatable.

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:20.023
A useful catalog records source owners, covered assets, event types, important fields,

00:01:20.023 --> 00:01:23.656
collection paths, parsing status, retention, access restrictions, cost,

00:01:23.656 --> 00:01:25.018
and dependent detections.

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:30.467
This documentation helps engineers assess the impact of changes and helps analysts

00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:34.100
find the best available evidence during an investigation.
